  "category": "Other",
  "tags": [
    "structural testing",
    "concrete",
    "loading"
  ],
  "estimated_time_mins": 12,
  "materials": [],
  "steps": [
    {
      "step_id": 0,
      "title": "Clean testing area",
      "instruction": "Remove debris and any obstacles from the test zone to ensure a safe working environment.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 1,
      "title": "Prepare collapsed airbag",
      "instruction": "Vacuum all air from the airbag, clamp it so it stays collapsed, and remove the clamps shortly after air begins to flow.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 2,
      "title": "Install PTFE sheets on sleepers",
      "instruction": "Lay greased double‑layer PTFE sheets onto the sleeper supports to provide a low‑friction surface.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 3,
      "title": "Position panel using crane",
      "instruction": "Using a crane, lift the concrete wall panel and slide it into place from the top onto the prepared sleepers.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 4,
      "title": "Install displacement and pressure sensors",
      "instruction": "Mount all required sensors, specifically displacement transducers and pressure sensors, at their designated locations on the panel and support structure.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 5,
      "title": "Measure panel dimensions",
      "instruction": "Record as‑built dimensions of the panel, including wall thicknesses and width along the entire length.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 6,
      "title": "Monitor sensors during axial pre‑load",
      "instruction": "Begin real‑time monitoring of all sensors while applying the pre‑test axial (dead) load.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 7,
      "title": "Apply and adjust dead load with rocker arms",
      "instruction": "Use the rocker‑arm assembly to apply the dead load that seats the panel into the lower reaction; note that the panel may shift on the sliding supports. Then remove the dead‑load assembly and re‑apply it using shimming plates on the anvil to obtain a tight fit, keeping the arm tops near vertical or slightly inclined toward the panel.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 8,
      "title": "Verify sensor functionality",
      "instruction": "Check that all sensors, especially the rocker‑arm load cells, are operating correctly before proceeding.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 9,
      "title": "Start data acquisition at appropriate sample rate",
      "instruction": "Begin collecting data for the test; set the sample rate high enough to capture the load rate that will be used later (see step 12).",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 10,
      "title": "Start air compressor to working pressure",
      "instruction": "Turn on the air compressor and allow it to reach its designated working pressure before inflating the airbag.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 11,
      "title": "Inflate airbag at controlled rate",
      "instruction": "Open the pneumatic manifold and apply pressure to the airbag at a rate of 2 psf per minute to ensure a static test. Alternatively, increase pressure to predefined stopping points and hold while sensors record.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 12,
      "title": "Ensure uniform bag inflation and monitor",
      "instruction": "Confirm that all airbags inflate to a similar pressure and continuously monitor bag pressure and panel deflection in real time.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 13,
      "title": "Record cracks and contact area periodically",
      "instruction": "Mark, measure, and document any cracks that develop; also record the airbag contact area using a ruler, tape measure, or similar device at regular intervals.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 14,
      "title": "Stop test at displacement or failure limit",
      "instruction": "Terminate the test when the airbag reaches its maximum displacement or load capacity (30 in. maximum in this study) or when the wall has sufficiently failed.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 15,
      "title": "Backup acquisition data",
      "instruction": "Save and back up all data from the data acquisition system.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 16,
      "title": "Unload rocker arms then release air pressure",
      "instruction": "Fully remove the rocker‑arm loads before depressurizing the airbag; then open the pneumatic manifold bleed valve or apply vacuum to rapidly remove the remaining air pressure.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "step_id": 17,
      "title": "Remove sensors, clean area, and extract panel",
      "instruction": "Clear away all sensors, clean the testing area, and lift the panel out of the apparatus.",
      "substeps": [],
      "is_critical": false,
      "timers": []
    }
  ],
  "verification_status": "literature_sourced",
  "normalised_at": "2026-06-19T01:26:46.114525Z",
  "llm_normalised": true,
  "description": "To assess the accuracy of the testing method, three total 8 in. thick solid section control specimens measuring 41 ft in length and 4 ft wide were fabricated to test load carrying capacity on slender panels as shown Fig. 22. This series of tests (labeled Solid 1, 2, and 3) were intended to allow the researchers to verify the testing setup on a set of specimens where the mechanics were well-known. Solid concrete tilt-up wall panels are very well understood [3,4] and utilize what is known as the Slender Wall Design Methodology [5]. Further, the Response 2000 moment-curvature-deflection program is often used to model the behavior of slender wall panels [6]. The results of solid panel tests are presented as load versus deflection curves using the test setup described herein where the total moment is calculated using Eq. (1), and deflection is determined from the method described in the previous section. Each of these tests is compared to the slender wall method and Response 2000 in Fig. 23.Fig. 22Cross-section of control panel (a) cross section, (b) axail load end assembly, and (c) bottom support end assembly.Fig 22Fig. 23Total Applied Moment versus Deflection for Solid Wall Panels plotted with Response 2000 results and Slender Wall Method.Fig 23",
  "content_type": "protocol",
  "content_type_score": 80,
  "exclude_low_confidence": false,
